platformd-verifyd NEWS
======================

platformd-verifyd 0.1.2

  * Verification is serialized per UID instead of across the entire machine,
    with a global limit of eight workers.
  * Workers have a CLOCK_BOOTTIME deadline of 120 seconds, configurable from 1
    to 600 seconds with PLATFORMD_VERIFY_TIMEOUT_SEC. A deadline terminates the
    worker and returns the new VerificationTimedOut Varlink error.
  * Disconnecting the requesting Varlink client terminates its worker,
    suppresses the success event to platformd-trustd, and releases the per-UID
    slot.

platformd-verifyd 0.1.1

  Audit hardening:

  * The PAM conversation runs in a forked worker, so a finger on the reader no
    longer blocks the event loop.
    The Varlink reply is sent when the worker exits.
  * One verification runs at a time: a concurrent VerifyUser is refused with the
    Busy error rather than queued invisibly against the same reader.
  * A request with no resolvable session is refused with NoSession up front,
    instead of running a verification that would refresh nothing.
  * The recorded event to platformd-trustd is built from a JSON variant (field
    values escaped) rather than string-formatted; the socket accepts either a
    runtime directory or a socket path; a stale socket is cleared on startup.

platformd-verifyd 0.1.0

  * First release of the user-presence verification service, the challenger in
    platform authentication. Over the Varlink interface io.platformd.Verify it
    runs the platformd-verify PAM stack for
    the calling user and, on success, records the verification with
    platformd-trustd so the session's freshness reflects it. The user is always
    the caller's own, taken from the peer credentials. verifyctl(1) drives it.
    Factor-agnostic: the factors are PAM modules the administrator stacks.
